{
  "raw": {
    "https://gallaudet.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/GUCareers/job/Gallaudet-University/Research-Operations-Coordinator_JR0000001344": {
      "title": "Research Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-03\nFull time\n\nOVERVIEW: Gallaudet University, federally chartered in 1864, is a bilingual, diverse, and multicultural institution of higher education dedicated to the intellectual and professional advancement of Deaf and hard of hearing individuals through American Sign Language and English. Gallaudet maintains a proud tradition of research and scholarly activity and prepares graduates for career opportunities in a highly competitive, technological, and rapidly changing world. As part of the university, the Laurent Clerc National Deaf Education Center is a federally funded center that operates exemplary elementary and secondary education programs for deaf and hard of hearing students as well as national outreach programs which develop and disseminate innovative curricula, instructional techniques, and products nationwide, and provide information, training, and technical assistance to parents and professionals serving children from birth through age 21.   Job Title: Research Operations Coordinator Worker Type: Regular   Pay Type: Salary   Exempt Status: Yes   Compensation Grade (Track/Level): P2   Hiring Range (Commensurate with experience and qualifications): $70,878.00-$94,469.00   Salary Ranges: All Compensation Grades   Supervisory Organization (Department): Deaf Trauma Informed Care Center   Summary: Reporting to the Director of Deaf and Hard of Hearing (DHH) Child Resilience Center, the Research Operations Coordinator coordinates administrative and financial operations, including grant management, personnel and procurement processes, communications and events, and compliance with institutional policies and sponsor requirements.   Job Description: Coordinates center events (e.g., training sessions, workshops, conferences) and provides effective coordination across research teams and university units; and serves as the  point of contact for operational matters. Coordinates center-related communications and marketing to promote programs and initiatives, ensuring alignment with institutional and center branding guidelines. Collaborates with the Principal Investigator (PI) and center leadership on identifying and procuring revenue-based contracts and partnerships to sustain center operations. Collaborates with the PI and center leadership on the implementation of strategic plans and community-based action plans that support long-term goals; and manages operational projects as assigned. Drafts and coordinates scopes of work, memoranda of understanding, and agreements for center activities, collaborations, and partnerships. Collaborates with the research team on preparing, drafting, and delivering presentation materials and reports. Maintains financial oversight of expenditures, encumbrances, and available balances for federal grants and foundation awards; and produces analytical reports and data visualizations to communicate budget status to PIs and stakeholders. Creates subaward and contractual agreements in coordination with the Office of Research and Innovation and the Office of Strategic Sourcing; monitors subrecipient invoices and expenditures; tracks vendor deliverables; validates expenses against contract terms; and maintains documentation for audit compliance. Supports research and programmatic project work as capacity allows, including language access and accessibility tasks such as participating on the translation team, reviewing translated materials, and tracking accessibility-related approvals. Processes new hire paperwork for students and staff; initiates setup or modifications of costing allocations for center personnel in the institutional financial system; and provides on-site supervision support as required. Engages in proactive problem solving with the team; and resolves administrative issues through coordination, reporting, analysis, and identification of solutions. Serves as a liaison between the center, subrecipients, and vendors to facilitate effective communication and timely issue resolution. Establishes and maintains a positive and supportive working relationship with co-workers and supervisors. Demonstrates a genuine commitment to belonging in the workplace and participates in activities and workshops to foster continuous learning. Serves as a mandatory reporter under Title IX; and commits to completing Title IX training and other compliance requirements on an annual basis. Prioritizes safety in the workplace as a collective responsibility for the well-being of employees and fosters a culture of safety that is integral to our success, including strict adherence to safety procedures and protocols, as well as promptly reporting any safety hazards or incidents. Performs related and other duties as needed. Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. A minimum of three years of professional experience with project management, research administration, event coordination, or a related field. Ability to communicate in American Sign Language. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree in Psychology, Social Work, Counseling, or a related field. Experience with federal grant administration.  Experience working in a higher education setting. Knowledge, Skills, And Abilities: Strong interpersonal and teamwork skills. Strong ability to organize and prioritize multiple concurrent responsibilities and deadlines with attention to detail. Demonstrated experience with federal grant administration and cost principles, including 2 CFR Part 200. Demonstrated experience with financial management, including budget tracking, expenditure monitoring, and financial reporting. Demonstrated ability to work with and across University departments, outside constituents and groups. Additional Job Details: This is a grant-funded position.   Other Important Information: The successful candidate will undergo a background check that must be cleared prior to working at Gallaudet University.   Current Employees (Regular Status and Extended Temporary) Please use the link below or the \"Find Jobs\" report directly within Workday. https://www.myworkday.com/gallaudet/d/inst/1$9925/9925$2685.htmld   Need help finding the right job? We can recommend jobs specifically for you! Create a custom Job Alert by selecting criteria that suit your career interests.   Gallaudet University is an equal opportunity employer/educational institution and does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, national origin, religion, age, hearing status, disability, genetic information, covered veteran status, marital status, personal appearance, sexual orientation, family responsibilities, matriculation, political affiliation, source of income, place of business or residence, pregnancy, childbirth, or any other unlawful basis. This policy is in compliance with Title VII of the Civil Rights Act, the Americans with Disabilities Act, the Rehabilitation Act, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, the District of Columbia Human Rights Act, and other applicable laws and applies to all procedures affecting applicants and employees including, but not necessarily limited to: recruitment, hiring, placement, promotion, transfer, reassignment, reappointment, tenure, demotion, selection for training, layoff, furlough, and termination, compensation, and all other conditions or privileges of employment."
    },
    "https://gallaudet.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/GUCareers/job/Gallaudet-University/Associate-Youth-Programs-Coordinator_JR0000001343": {
      "title": "Associate Youth Programs Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nOVERVIEW: Gallaudet University, federally chartered in 1864, is a bilingual, diverse, and multicultural institution of higher education dedicated to the intellectual and professional advancement of Deaf and hard of hearing individuals through American Sign Language and English. Gallaudet maintains a proud tradition of research and scholarly activity and prepares graduates for career opportunities in a highly competitive, technological, and rapidly changing world. As part of the university, the Laurent Clerc National Deaf Education Center is a federally funded center that operates exemplary elementary and secondary education programs for deaf and hard of hearing students as well as national outreach programs which develop and disseminate innovative curricula, instructional techniques, and products nationwide, and provide information, training, and technical assistance to parents and professionals serving children from birth through age 21.   Job Title: Associate Youth Programs Coordinator Worker Type: Extended Temporary (Fixed Term)   Pay Type: Salary   Exempt Status: Yes   Compensation Grade (Track/Level): A1   Hiring Range (Commensurate with experience and qualifications): $46,462.00-$61,915.00   Salary Ranges: All Compensation Grades   Supervisory Organization (Department): Youth Programs   Summary: Reporting to the Outreach Manager, the Associate Youth Programs Coordinator plans and coordinates Gallaudet’s year-round youth and summer programs, managing operational details and serving as the primary point of contact for campers, families, instructors, and staff.   Job Description: Coordinates the day-to-day planning and delivery of summer programs/camps, tracking tasks against the annual summer program calendar and flagging delays or gaps to the team. Coordinates camper registration and data through Slate and other university systems, ensuring registration forms, medical release forms, and waivers are current, accurate, and complete. Coordinates housing and dorm logistics with Residence Life, including room assignments, housing charts, check-in/check-out schedules, and bulletin board and signage setup. Compiles and maintains camper medical information, including medication authorizations, allergies, dietary restrictions, insurance information, and activity restrictions, and coordinates with health services and camp nursing team to keep records current. Coordinates transportation logistics for arrival/departure days and field trips, including transportation forms, vehicle reservations, and driver authorizations. Supports recruitment, interviewing, and onboarding of camp counselors and student workers, including background checks and payroll processing. Assists in developing and delivering training and orientation, including CPR/First Aid coordination, Title IX training scheduling, employment handbooks, and orientation materials. Coordinates procurement of camp materials, supplies, equipment, and promotional items, and manages vendor relationships (e.g., catering, transportation, and activity vendors) to support camp delivery. Tracks program-level camp expenditures and maintains accurate records, providing regular budget updates. Coordinates with campus units involved in camp delivery, including Housing, Dining Services, Department of Public Safety, and Information Technology, to resolve logistical issues at the program level. Maintains an active and on-brand social media presence for summer programs, developing and posting camp-related content in coordination with assigned staff or student workers. Develops camp, visual, and daily staff schedules, updating them as needed throughout each session. Serves as a responsive point of contact for campers, families, instructors, and camp staff, addressing day-to-day questions and concerns. Documents camp processes, timelines, and lessons learned to support planning and continuity for future summers. Provides support to other Youth Programs competitions (e.g., Academic Bowl, Battle of the Books, National Literary Competition) as needed, particularly during peak planning periods. Establishes and maintains a positive and supportive working relationship with co-workers and supervisors. Demonstrates a genuine commitment to belonging in the workplace and participates in activities and workshops to foster continuous learning. Serves as a mandatory reporter under Title IX; and commits to completing Title IX training and other compliance requirements on an annual basis. Prioritizes safety in the workplace as a collective responsibility for the well-being of employees and fosters a culture of safety that is integral to our success, including strict adherence to safety procedures and protocols, as well as promptly reporting any safety hazards or incidents. Performs related and other duties as needed. Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree. A minimum of two years of experience with student services, recruitment and outreach, or a related field (college student work and/or internship experience will be considered). Ability to communicate in American Sign Language. Preferred Qualifications: Experience working in a K-12 or higher education setting. Knowledge, Skills, And Abilities: Strong ability to write clearly and professionally. Strong ability to organize, communicate, present, and negotiate effectively. Strong ability to understand and communicate University programs and services. Demonstrated experience using data management systems for tracking and reporting. Demonstrated ability to work with and across University departments, outside constituents and groups. Additional Position Details: This a gift-funded position. This is a one-year extended temporary position with certain benefits including health insurance, paid vacation and sick time, and paid holidays. The extended temporary appointment may be renewed for another year or has an opportunity to convert to regular status with full benefits. If the successful candidate possesses three years of required professional experience, they may be hired as “Youth Programs Coordinator” at the compensation grade of A2. The incumbent must be able to travel or work on weekends as needed.   Other Important Information: The successful candidate will undergo a background check that must be cleared prior to working at Gallaudet University.   Current Employees (Regular Status and Extended Temporary) Please use the link below or the \"Find Jobs\" report directly within Workday. https://www.myworkday.com/gallaudet/d/inst/1$9925/9925$2683.htmld   Need help finding the right job? We can recommend jobs specifically for you! Create a custom Job Alert by selecting criteria that suit your career interests.   Gallaudet University is an equal opportunity employer/educational institution and does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, national origin, religion, age, hearing status, disability, genetic information, covered veteran status, marital status, personal appearance, sexual orientation, family responsibilities, matriculation, political affiliation, source of income, place of business or residence, pregnancy, childbirth, or any other unlawful basis. This policy is in compliance with Title VII of the Civil Rights Act, the Americans with Disabilities Act, the Rehabilitation Act, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, the District of Columbia Human Rights Act, and other applicable laws and applies to all procedures affecting applicants and employees including, but not necessarily limited to: recruitment, hiring, placement, promotion, transfer, reassignment, reappointment, tenure, demotion, selection for training, layoff, furlough, and termination, compensation, and all other conditions or privileges of employment."
    },
    "https://gallaudet.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/GUCareers/job/Clerc-Center/Project-Manager--Research-_JR0000001341": {
      "title": "Project Manager (Research)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nOVERVIEW: Gallaudet University, federally chartered in 1864, is a bilingual, diverse, and multicultural institution of higher education dedicated to the intellectual and professional advancement of Deaf and hard of hearing individuals through American Sign Language and English. Gallaudet maintains a proud tradition of research and scholarly activity and prepares graduates for career opportunities in a highly competitive, technological, and rapidly changing world. As part of the university, the Laurent Clerc National Deaf Education Center is a federally funded center that operates exemplary elementary and secondary education programs for deaf and hard of hearing students as well as national outreach programs which develop and disseminate innovative curricula, instructional techniques, and products nationwide, and provide information, training, and technical assistance to parents and professionals serving children from birth through age 21.   Job Title: Project Manager (Research) Worker Type: Regular   Pay Type: Salary   Exempt Status: Yes   Compensation Grade (Track/Level): CP2   Hiring Range (Commensurate with experience and qualifications):   Salary Ranges: All Compensation Grades   Supervisory Organization (Department): Monitoring, Evaluation and Research   Summary: Reporting to the Director of Monitoring, Evaluation, and Research, the Project Manager (Research) manages research activities in compliance with the Education of the Deaf Act, with special emphasis on educators, professionals, and families of deaf and hard of hearing students, including those who represent traditionally underserved groups.   Job Description: Manages research, evaluation, public input, and strategic planning processes in accordance of the Education of the Deaf Act, with special emphasis on educators, professionals, and families of students, including those who represent traditionally underserved groups. Manages identified research projects from project definition through development and supports dissemination and evaluation efforts as appropriate; provides research project leadership and actively manages research project plans; determines project goals, budgets, and timelines in collaboration with and/or based on input from related Clerc Center units and stakeholder groups; recommends and maintains timelines for identified projects; and facilitates a multi-year planning process. Manages the planning and implementation of evaluation and research project systems, mechanisms, and programs for the Clerc Center’s primary functions, including strategic planning, project development, research project management, public input gathering and dissemination, and reporting. Manages research projects related to Clerc Center priorities and initiatives with emphasis on the implementation of research-based practices for use by parents of deaf and hard of hearing children from birth through high school and professionals who work with them. Manages multiple responsibilities simultaneously; leads or participates on research project teams; and uses a collaborative process to define the specific roles and responsibilities for research team members. Works with team members to establish and follow procedures to protect the integrity of collected and sensitive data in compliance with federal and other compliance requirements; and follows professional and ethical standards established for evaluators and researchers. Uses advancing technologies to enhance the Clerc Center’s ability to achieve its established agenda and strategic plan goals, including resource development and dissemination activities. Establishes and maintains collaborations with internal and external stakeholders and researchers focused on the education of deaf and hard of hearing students and related priority areas; serves as an active, contributing member of the Clerc Center community and Gallaudet University as a whole; and serves as a partner in the Clerc Center as the national model for best practices in the education of deaf and hard of hearing children as well as the producer of rigorous and appropriate educational research. Establishes and maintains professional, collegial relationships with team members, supervisor program staff, school professionals, and potential collaborators with a genuine commitment to belonging for all involved. Works with other members of the unit to systematically document organizational planning progress for reporting, historical, and other purposes; and prepares clear and accurate reports tailored to meet the information needs of different audiences, including federal and University oversight groups, educators, parents, and researchers.  Maintains fiscal accountability for assigned budgets; and manages assigned resources and performs administrative responsibilities in compliance with policies, procedures, and timelines.  Ensures initiatives and projects provide support for the Clerc Center’s efforts to raise student achievement, with particular focus on deaf and hard of hearing students from birth through high school who have been traditionally underserved. Keeps abreast of legislative, educational, and research issues and trends that affect the education and research of deaf and hard of hearing children as well as impact Clerc Center’s national work. As assigned, supervises external contractors, and/or graduate students. Establishes and maintains a positive and supportive working relationship with co-workers and supervisors. Demonstrates a genuine commitment to belonging in the workplace and participates in activities and workshops to foster continuous learning. Serves as a mandatory reporter under Title IX; and commits to completing Title IX training and other compliance requirements on an annual basis. Prioritizes safety in the workplace as a collective responsibility for the well-being of employees and fosters a culture of safety that is integral to our success, including strict adherence to safety procedures and protocols, as well as promptly reporting any safety hazards or incidents. Performs related and other duties as needed. Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. A minimum of three years of professional experience with research activities, evaluation and assessment, or a related field. Ability to communicate in American Sign Language. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree. Experience working in a K-12 setting. Knowledge, Skills, And Abilities: Strong knowledge with data programs for data analysis and reporting. Strong knowledge in research and/or evaluation design, methodology, data analysis, and data interpretation. Strong knowledge with professional standards related to the protection of human subjects and ethical and professional practice. Strong knowledge with technology for research, presentation, data visualization,meetings, and other project-related activities. Demonstrated experience with effective research knowledge translation for writing, presenting, and developing research products designed for families and education professionals. Demonstrated ability to work with and across University departments, outside constituents and groups.   Other Important Information: The successful candidate will undergo a background check that must be cleared prior to working at Gallaudet University.   Current Employees (Regular Status and Extended Temporary) Please use the link below or the \"Find Jobs\" report directly within Workday. https://www.myworkday.com/gallaudet/d/inst/1$9925/9925$2675.htmld   Need help finding the right job? We can recommend jobs specifically for you! Create a custom Job Alert by selecting criteria that suit your career interests.   Gallaudet University is an equal opportunity employer/educational institution and does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, national origin, religion, age, hearing status, disability, genetic information, covered veteran status, marital status, personal appearance, sexual orientation, family responsibilities, matriculation, political affiliation, source of income, place of business or residence, pregnancy, childbirth, or any other unlawful basis. This policy is in compliance with Title VII of the Civil Rights Act, the Americans with Disabilities Act, the Rehabilitation Act, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, the District of Columbia Human Rights Act, and other applicable laws and applies to all procedures affecting applicants and employees including, but not necessarily limited to: recruitment, hiring, placement, promotion, transfer, reassignment, reappointment, tenure, demotion, selection for training, layoff, furlough, and termination, compensation, and all other conditions or privileges of employment."
    },
    "https://gallaudet.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/GUCareers/job/Clerc-Center/Long-Term-Aide--MSSD-_JR0000001336": {
      "title": "Long Term Aide (MSSD)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nOVERVIEW: Gallaudet University, federally chartered in 1864, is a bilingual, diverse, and multicultural institution of higher education dedicated to the intellectual and professional advancement of Deaf and hard of hearing individuals through American Sign Language and English. Gallaudet maintains a proud tradition of research and scholarly activity and prepares graduates for career opportunities in a highly competitive, technological, and rapidly changing world. As part of the university, the Laurent Clerc National Deaf Education Center is a federally funded center that operates exemplary elementary and secondary education programs for deaf and hard of hearing students as well as national outreach programs which develop and disseminate innovative curricula, instructional techniques, and products nationwide, and provide information, training, and technical assistance to parents and professionals serving children from birth through age 21.   Job Title: Long Term Aide (MSSD) Worker Type: Fixed Term (Fixed Term)   Pay Type: Hourly   Exempt Status: No   Supervisory Organization (Department): Aides - JM   Summary: Supporting Clerc Center for a fixed-term basis.   Job Description: Primary Responsibilities: To provide and work with teachers to ensure the safety and well-being of the students working one-on-one while helping those master skills and improve academic performance in K-12 school settings. Familiarity and be able to react quickly and appropriately in crisis situations, vigilant in monitoring students and be agile, work with students with various instructional activities, classroom and student management.   Qualifications: High School diploma or GED required Two years of post-secondary education in education, deaf education, or a related field. Must have at least one year of experience working with children in K-12 educational setting. Knowledge and understanding of K-12 students and behaviors of students at different age groups Fluency in American Sign Language    Application Information: Up-to-date Resume Employment Information: Employment Dates: ASAP – June 11, 2027 The contractual period is divided into two portions: August 13, 2026 - December 18, 2026 and January 4, 2027  – June 11, 2027, and is dependent upon performance and program needs.   Required Orientation Training:  to be determined All employees hired must undergo and pay for a background investigation if selected for the job and must be cleared before starting first day on the job.  Fees for the background investigation can range from $35.50 to $135 dependent on your location(s). Background Check and Gallaudet Student Conduct Office Records must be in good standing. Compensation:  $18.40 per hour Work Days: All student days, except for holiday breaks, spring break, and professional days. If there is a professional day requiring temporary employees to attend, employees will be notified. Work Hours:  Mondays - Fridays 7:30am – 4:00pm, including a 30-minute unpaid break. Deadline:  ASAP If you have any questions, please contact Clerc Center Human Resources Office at Clerchr@gallaudet.edu .   Other Important Information: The successful candidate will undergo a background check that must be cleared prior to working at Gallaudet University.   Current Employees (Regular Status and Extended Temporary) Please use the link below or the \"Find Jobs\" report directly within Workday. https://www.myworkday.com/gallaudet/d/inst/1$9925/9925$2673.htmld   Need help finding the right job? We can recommend jobs specifically for you! Create a custom Job Alert by selecting criteria that suit your career interests.   Gallaudet University is an equal opportunity employer/educational institution and does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, national origin, religion, age, hearing status, disability, genetic information, covered veteran status, marital status, personal appearance, sexual orientation, family responsibilities