    "https://gallaudet.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/GUCareers/job/Clerc-Center/Teacher--MSSD---Science-_JR0000001218": {
      "title": "Teacher (MSSD - Science)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nOVERVIEW: Gallaudet University, federally chartered in 1864, is a bilingual, diverse, and multicultural institution of higher education dedicated to the intellectual and professional advancement of Deaf and hard of hearing individuals through American Sign Language and English. Gallaudet maintains a proud tradition of research and scholarly activity and prepares graduates for career opportunities in a highly competitive, technological, and rapidly changing world. As part of the university, the Laurent Clerc National Deaf Education Center is a federally funded center that operates exemplary elementary and secondary education programs for deaf and hard of hearing students as well as national outreach programs which develop and disseminate innovative curricula, instructional techniques, and products nationwide, and provide information, training, and technical assistance to parents and professionals serving children from birth through age 21.   Job Title: Teacher (MSSD - Science) Worker Type: Regular   Pay Type: Salary   Exempt Status: Yes   Compensation Grade (Track/Level): CF2   Hiring Range (Commensurate with experience and qualifications):   Salary Ranges: All Compensation Grades   Supervisory Organization (Department): Academic Programs   Summary: Plans and implements and researches effective instructional practices that maximize student learning; develops instructional plans/units that incorporate the national mission priorities of literacy, transition, and family involvement; collaborates with other educators on teams/departments to provide an effective instructional program; documents and monitors student progress; communicates with families regarding student progress; maintains professional growth; and is accountable for student achievement and safety, and engages in action research for continuous improvement of instruction.   Job Description: PRINCIPAL ACCOUNTABILITIES: Plans, implements, and researches effective instructional practices that maximize student learning. Ensures the physical and emotional safety and supervision of all students; assists with the supervision of students at all times. Plans units, projects, and daily lessons collaboratively with team/department members; incorporates activities into daily teaching that include student-centered learning, the KDES and MSSD student outcomes, literacy development, family involvement, and transition skills. Maintains high expectations of all students; including students who are from traditionally underserved populations – those who are lower achieving academically, who come from families who speak a language other than English in the home, who have additional disabilities, who are members of diverse racial or cultural groups, who are from rural areas, or who have unique educational needs; communicates class and course expectations to students and families (i.e. course syllabus). Ensures that the curriculum is aligned with the standards and benchmarks in all outcome areas. Implements plans in the classroom by providing effective instruction that is aligned with the school improvement plan to maximize student growth in reading, writing, emotional intelligence, and mathematics. Provides instruction that reflects an understanding of different cultural backgrounds and family needs. Presents a program of instruction that is designed to meet the needs of individual students with appropriate accommodations for interests, disabilities, special talents, learning styles, and pace of learning. Establishes and maintains a learning environment that is conducive to student learning; establishes a nurturing relationship with students. Uses appropriate disciplinary approaches with intent of teaching students appropriate behavior. Conducts on-going assessment of student progress; uses a variety of appropriate assessments, including standardized tests and portfolios, to document student performance. Analyzes data from classroom and school-wide assessments; uses this information to modify lessons and instruction to increase instructional effectiveness. Develops IEP/IFSP based on assessment information; turns goals and objectives into classroom instruction; reviews IEP/IFSP regularly to monitor student progress toward meeting goals/objectives; provides quarterly reports about student progress.    Participates in meetings including, but not limited to, SRTs, IEPs/IFSPs, Back to School Night and school-wide meetings (school-wide meetings may last until 4:30 p.m. and teachers are expected to stay for the entire meeting). Communicates information regarding student progress to families; completes all forms/reports related to student progress to be sent home to families. Maintains appropriate records on students, including attendance, grades, behavior incidents, progress toward meeting IEPs/IFSPs goals and objectives, communications with parents, and student meetings. Adheres to institutional policies, procedures, and Clerc Center expectations, including compliance with work hours (teachers are expected to work the hours necessary to complete their job - minimally this is 7 hours per teacher work day); completes all leave slips and other forms in accordance with guidelines. Participates in Training and Professional Development activities; remains knowledgeable of the current trends and effective practices in area of expertise or content area; maintains professional certifications and portfolio (minimally teachers are expected to maintain the equivalent of certifications through professional development workshops and training activities); applies newly learned information to classroom instruction. Provides opportunities for visitors to observe classroom activities and share information; provides training for practicum and internship students. Maintains a professional relationship with other team/department members, parents, and the community; works collaboratively with educators from various units. Engages in action research through a spiraling cycle of problem identification, systematic data collection, reflection, analysis, data-driven action taken, and problem redefinition. Proposes effective, innovative strategies which have resulted from action research to be disseminated to other programs and educators. Documents all national mission contacts and activities using the “Contact Log” and “Activity Summary” databases, according to established procedures and timelines. Works to support the Clerc Center mission in alignment with organizational priorities and the Clerc Center Strategic Plan. Serves as an active, contributing member of the Clerc Center community and Gallaudet University as a whole. Establishes and maintains a positive and supportive working relationship with co-workers and supervisors. Demonstrates a genuine commitment to belonging in the workplace and participates in activities and workshops to foster continuous learning. Serves as a mandatory reporter under Title IX; and commits to completing Title IX training and other compliance requirements on an annual basis. Prioritizes safety in the workplace as a collective responsibility for the well-being of employees and fosters a culture of safety that is integral to our success, including strict adherence to safety procedures and protocols, as well as promptly reporting any safety hazards or incidents. Performs related and other duties as needed. SPECIFICATIONS: MSSD Science Department— requires a Master’s degree in Deaf Education or the Science and/or STEAM Education content area (if the Master’s degree is in Deaf Education, the Bachelor’s degree must be in the Science and/or STEAM Education content area or the candidate must present proof of state certification in the Science content area). Teaching Fellow— Requires bachelor’s degree in the Science and/or STEAM Education content, deaf education or education, have minimal teaching experience, be enrolled or eligible to enroll in a graduate program in the content area, Deaf Education or Education at any greater Washington D.C. area university or college that is accredited. Teaching out of the Classroom (TOC)— This program is for eligible Clerc Center employees who do not meet the requirements for teaching fellows. This is a \"pre-teaching fellow\" position, available for a limited time, during which eligible applicants are required to obtain the necessary degree to qualify for promotion as a teaching fellow or teacher. To be eligible for the TOC opportunity: 1. Have a Bachelor's Degree in any content area 2. A letter of endorsement from the current supervisor 3. Meet the minimum number of years of continuous employment at the Clerc Center as outlined below: Daily Substitute Teacher: minimum of 1,200 hours, equivalent to 1 year Short-Term Substitute Teacher: equivalent to a minimum of 1 year Long-Term Extended Temporary Teacher: a minimum of 1 year Teacher Aide: minimum of 2 years and documented evidence of leading activities. Long-Term Aide: minimum of 2 years and documented evidence of leading activities.  ALL POSITIONS REQUIRE: • Knowledge and understanding of child development and/or adolescent needs • Ability to provide/implement appropriate instructional activities for secondary school students • Ability to communicate in American Sign Language APPLICATION INFORMATION: • Letter of interest • Up-to-date Resume • Copies of all valid licenses to teach (if you possess any) • A copy of unofficial transcripts of all degrees EMPLOYMENT INFORMATION: • All employees hired must undergo a background investigation if selected for the job and must be cleared before starting first day on the job. Reports from Background Check and Gallaudet Conduct & Security Office must be in a good standing. • Compensation: According to Teacher pay schedule. • Hours: Full Day – 8:15am – 3:45pm • Deadline: Until Filled ADDITIONAL INFORMATION: Any vacant position that will be filled is dependent upon resignations received and funding available. If you get the job offer, we will require official transcripts before the final hiring process is being finalized and approved.   Other Important Information: The successful candidate will undergo a background check that must be cleared prior to working at Gallaudet University.   Current Employees (Regular Status and Extended Temporary) Please use the link below or the \"Find Jobs\" report directly within Workday. https://www.myworkday.com/gallaudet/d/inst/1$9925/9925$2695.htmld   Need help finding the right job? We can recommend jobs specifically for you! Create a custom Job Alert by selecting criteria that suit your career interests.   Gallaudet University is an equal opportunity employer/educational institution and does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, national origin, religion, age, hearing status, disability, genetic information, covered veteran status, marital status, personal appearance, sexual orientation, family responsibilities, matriculation, political affiliation, source of income, place of business or residence, pregnancy, childbirth, or any other unlawful basis. This policy is in compliance with Title VII of the Civil Rights Act, the Americans with Disabilities Act, the Rehabilitation Act, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, the District of Columbia Human Rights Act, and other applicable laws and applies to all procedures affecting applicants and employees including, but not necessarily limited to: recruitment, hiring, placement, promotion, transfer, reassignment, reappointment, tenure, demotion, selection for training, layoff, furlough, and termination, compensation, and all other conditions or privileges of employment."
    },
    "https://gallaudet.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/GUCareers/job/Gallaudet-University/General-Counsel_JR0000001359-1": {
      "title": "General Counsel",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nOVERVIEW: Gallaudet University, federally chartered in 1864, is a bilingual, diverse, and multicultural institution of higher education dedicated to the intellectual and professional advancement of Deaf and hard of hearing individuals through American Sign Language and English. Gallaudet maintains a proud tradition of research and scholarly activity and prepares graduates for career opportunities in a highly competitive, technological, and rapidly changing world. As part of the university, the Laurent Clerc National Deaf Education Center is a federally funded center that operates exemplary elementary and secondary education programs for deaf and hard of hearing students as well as national outreach programs which develop and disseminate innovative curricula, instructional techniques, and products nationwide, and provide information, training, and technical assistance to parents and professionals serving children from birth through age 21.   Job Title: General Counsel Worker Type: Regular   Pay Type: Salary   Exempt Status: Yes   Compensation Grade (Track/Level): E2   Hiring Range (Commensurate with experience and qualifications): $183,582.00-$244,776.00   Salary Ranges: All Compensation Grades   Supervisory Organization (Department): Office of the General Counsel   Summary: Reporting to the President, the General Counsel serves as Gallaudet University's principal legal advisor, including for Clerc Center, and provides strategic counsel on institutional strategy, legal matters, governance, compliance, litigation, and legal risk management to protect the University's interests and advance its mission.   Job Description: Legal Advice and Counsel Advises the President, Board of Trustees, Executive Team, and other University leadership on legal matters, institutional governance, policies, and strategic decisions. Interprets applicable federal, state, and local laws and regulations, including those affecting higher education, civil rights, employment, privacy, research, contracts, and intellectual property. Prepares and reviews legal reports, correspondence, presentations, and materials for the Executive Team and the Board of Trustees. Coordinates legal matters across the University and provides practical guidance on complex, controversial, and high-profile issues. Litigation, Contracts, and Risk Management Directs the University's legal representation in litigation, administrative proceedings, investigations, claims, and disputes. Reviews University contracts and agreements involving employment, procurement, real estate, technology, sponsored research, external sales, and other institutional activities. Assesses legal risks and develops strategies to protect the University's interests, prevent disputes, and ensure compliance with applicable laws, regulations, and policies. Establishes and maintains appropriate legal safeguards and internal controls while promoting consistent application of University policies and procedures. Supports a culture of compliance, restorative justice, and conflict resolution when violations occur, whether actual or perceived. Leadership and Resource Management Provides supervision to staff; provides ongoing feedback to all employees, including student workers; completes the annual performance evaluations for each regular status and extended temporary staff; sets goals and expectations for each staff; resolves workplace conflicts in a timely manner and seeks support from Human Resources or the Office of Equal Opportunity Program, if needed; promotes a safe and healthy work environment; and ensures professional development opportunities are provided to each regular status and extended temporary staff. Ensures the delivery of high-quality, timely, and cost-effective legal services through effective management of internal resources and outside counsel. Manages outside counsel selection, engagement, contracts, and budgets. Develops and oversees the legal budget and provides reports to the President and, as appropriate, the Board of Trustees. Promotes legal awareness across campus through training, clear guidance, and collaboration with university leaders and administrators. Institutional and Professional Engagement Builds collaborative relationships with faculty, staff, students, trustees, alumni, donors, government agencies, community leaders, and external partners. Monitors national legal developments affecting higher education and consults with relevant professional associations and legal organizations. Maintains membership in the National Association of College and University Attorneys and other organizations as requested. Additional Responsibilities Establishes and maintains a positive and supportive working relationship with co-workers and supervisors. Demonstrates a genuine commitment to belonging in the workplace and participates in activities and workshops to foster continuous learning. Serves as a mandatory reporter under Title IX; and commits to completing Title IX training and other compliance requirements on an annual basis. Prioritizes safety in the workplace as a collective responsibility for the well-being of employees and fosters a culture of safety that is integral to our success, including strict adherence to safety procedures and protocols, as well as promptly reporting any safety hazards or incidents.  Performs related and other duties as needed. Required Qualifications: Juris Doctor (J.D.) degree. A minimum of six years of professional experience with legal administration, policy development, or a related field.  Ability to communicate in American Sign Language. Preferred Qualifications: Supervisory experience. Experience working in a higher education setting. Knowledge, Skills, And Abilities: Strong ability to analyze complex issues, exercise sound judgment, and communicate effectively through written and presentation skills while maintaining high ethical standards. Demonstrated knowledge of federal, state, and local civil rights laws and regulations, including the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Demonstrated experience leading legal professionals and providing strategic legal counsel to leadership on complex, controversial, and high-profile matters. Demonstrated experience with or knowledge of higher education, including faculty and staff relations, academic policies, and institutional governance. Demonstrated ability to work with and across University departments, outside constituents, and groups. Additional Position Details: The successful candidate must be able to obtain or possess a law license in the District of Columbia within one year of employment.   Other Important Information: The successful candidate will undergo a background check that must be cleared prior to working at Gallaudet University.   Current Employees (Regular Status and Extended Temporary) Please use the link below or the \"Find Jobs\" report directly within Workday. https://www.myworkday.com/gallaudet/d/inst/1$9925/9925$2703.htmld   Need help finding the right job? We can recommend jobs specifically for you! Create a custom Job Alert by selecting criteria that suit your career interests.   Gallaudet University is an equal opportunity employer/educational institution and does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, national origin, religion, age, hearing status, disability, genetic information, covered veteran status, marital status, personal appearance, sexual orientation, family responsibilities, matriculation, political affiliation, source of income, place of business or residence, pregnancy, childbirth, or any other unlawful basis. This policy is in compliance with Title VII of the Civil Rights Act, the Americans with Disabilities Act, the Rehabilitation Act, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, the District of Columbia Human Rights Act, and other applicable laws and applies to all procedures affecting applicants and employees including, but not necessarily limited to: recruitment, hiring, placement, promotion, transfer, reassignment, reappointment, tenure, demotion, selection for training, layoff, furlough, and termination, compensation, and all other conditions or privileges of employment."
    },
    "https://gallaudet.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/GUCareers/job/Gallaudet-University/Assistant-Director--Development-_JR0000001357": {
      "title": "Assistant Director (Development)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nOVERVIEW: Gallaudet University, federally chartered in 1864, is a bilingual, diverse, and multicultural institution of higher education dedicated to the intellectual and professional advancement of Deaf and hard of hearing individuals through American Sign Language and English. Gallaudet maintains a proud tradition of research and scholarly activity and prepares graduates for career opportunities in a highly competitive, technological, and rapidly changing world. As part of the university, the Laurent Clerc National Deaf Education Center is a federally funded center that operates exemplary elementary and secondary education programs for deaf and hard of hearing students as well as national outreach programs which develop and disseminate innovative curricula, instructional techniques, and products nationwide, and provide information, training, and technical assistance to parents and professionals serving children from birth through age 21.   Job Title: Assistant Director (Development) Worker Type: Regular   Pay Type: Salary   Exempt Status: Yes   Compensation Grade (Track/Level): P2   Hiring Range (Commensurate with experience and qualifications): $70,878.00-$94,469.00   Salary Ranges: All Compensation Grades   Supervisory Organization (Department): Fundraising   Summary: Reporting to the Executive Director of Institutional Advancement, the Assistant Director of Development plays a key role in advancing the university’s mission by securing private support from individuals, foundations, and organizations.   Job Description: Builds and manages a portfolio of donors and prospects that includes alumni, major gift donors, and/or corporations and foundations.  Guides constituents through the fundraising cycle to generate partnerships that include major gifts, deferred giving, corporate sponsorships, and grants.  Aligns external support with institutional priorities through research, proposal writing, and strategic donor engagement. Maintains a portfolio of donors and constituents and cultivates relationships with each of them, and solicits them. Develops, implements, and evaluates fund development portfolio and portfolio strategies that will meaningfully engage donors. Leads and organizes donor visits and meetings for the purpose of qualifying, cultivating, and soliciting gifts and grants to the University; and travels to meet with prospective funders as needed. Writes proposals and grants in support of institutional priorities and aligned with the University’s strategic plans. Conducts significant discovery work through targeted research and review of published funder guidelines and donor priorities to build a portfolio that includes constituents capable of making major investments in the University. Identifies prospects on an ongoing basis; and works with guidance and direction to design and implement strategies to move donors through the fundraising cycle toward solicitation by identifying, qualifying, cultivating, and stewarding them. Drafts, reviews, and edits complex proposals, letters, gift agreements, and reports; compiles supporting documentation to comply with submission requirements and deadlines; and prepares briefing materials for the leadership team as necessary. Maintains an accurate gifts/grants calendar, including the submission and tracking of proposals, subsequent awards, and reports. Consults subject-matter experts among faculty and program administration to understand and develop information documents to share with constituencies about the university and its need for private support.  Assists faculty and staff in preparing and submitting applications for research or program funding. Travels nationwide as needed for effective relationship management and to keep Gallaudet top of mind.    Hosts campus visits for donors and related constituents and either participates in and/or arranges visits to donor sites for campus leadership. Participates in activities and workshops to foster continuous learning. Participates in University-wide development activities as needed. Establishes and maintains a positive and supportive working relationship with co-workers and supervisors. Demonstrates a genuine commitment to belonging in the workplace and participates in activities and workshops to foster continuous learning. Serves as a mandatory reporter under Title IX; and commits to completing Title IX training and other compliance requirements on an annual basis. Prioritizes safety in the workplace as a collective responsibility for the well-being of employees and fosters a culture of safety that is integral to our success, including strict adherence to safety procedures and protocols, as well as promptly reporting any safety hazards or incidents. Performs related and other duties as needed. Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. A minimum of three years of professional experience with business operations, project management, community engagement, sales and fundraising, or a related field. Ability to communicate in American Sign Language. Preferred Qualifications Experience working in a higher education setting.  Knowledge, Skills, And Abilities: Strong technological and database literacy. Strong communication and interpersonal skills. Strong ability to develop well-crafted, clear, and concise professional presentations. Strong ability to work independently and exercise tact and good judgment. Strong ability to synthesize and articulate the priorities of the university and match them with the values and interests of prospects.  Demonstrated ability to work with and across University departments, outside constituents, and groups. Additional Job Details: This is a revenue-funded position.   Other Important Information: The successful candidate will undergo a background check that must be cleared prior to working at Gallaudet University.   Current Employees (Regular Status and Extended Temporary) Please use the link below or the \"Find Jobs\" report directly within Workday. https://www.myworkday.com/gallaudet/d/inst/1$9925/9925$2701.htmld   Need help finding the right job? We can recommend jobs specifically for you! Create a custom Job Alert by selecting criteria that suit your career interests.   Gallaudet University is an equal opportunity employer/educational institution and does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, national origin, religion, age, hearing status, disability, genetic information, covered veteran status, marital status, personal appearance, sexual orientation, family responsibilities, matriculation, political affiliation, source of income, place of business or residence, pregnancy, childbirth, or any other unlawful basis. This policy is in compliance with Title VII of the Civil Rights Act, the Americans with Disabilities Act, the Rehabilitation Act, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, the District of Columbia Human Rights Act, and other applicable laws and applies to all procedures affecting applicants and employees including, but not necessarily limited to: recruitment, hiring, placement, promotion, transfer, reassignment, reappointment, tenure, demotion, selection for training, layoff, furlough, and termination, compensation, and all other conditions or privileges of employment."
    },
    "https://gallaudet.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/GUCareers/job/Gallaudet-University/Major-Gifts-Officer_JR0000001358": {
      "title": "Major Gifts Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nOVERVIEW: Gallaudet University, federally chartered in 1864, is a bilingual, diverse, and multicultural institution of higher education dedicated to the intellectual and professional advancement of Deaf and hard of hearing individuals through American Sign Language and English. Gallaudet maintains a proud tradition of research and scholarly activity and prepares graduates for career opportunities in a highly competitive, technological, and rapidly changing world. As part of the university, the Laurent Clerc National Deaf Education Center is a federally funded center that operates exemplary elementary and secondary education programs for deaf and hard of hearing students as well as national outreach programs which develop and disseminate innovative curricula, instructional techniques, and products nationwide, and provide information, training, and technical assistance to parents and professionals serving children from birth through age 21.   Job Title: Major Gifts Officer Worker Type: Regular   Pay Type: Salary   Exempt Status: Yes   Compensation Grade (Track/Level): P3   Hiring Range (Commensurate with experience and qualifications): $85,095.00-$113,425.00   Salary Ranges: All Compensation Grades   Supervisory Organization (Department): Fundraising   Summary: Reporting to the Executive Director of Institutional Advancement, the Major Gifts Officer plans, initiates and manages appropriate activities for securing gifts from individual major donors.   Job Description: Consults and determines fundraising goals for the fiscal year and establishes activities and strategies to achieve these goals based on a portfolio of major donors that includes corporations, foundations, and individuals; and ensures focus on acquiring private support is directly tied to Gallaudet priorities that are currently outlined in the Gallaudet Strategic Plan. Identifies prospects on an ongoing basis; works with guidance and direction from the Executive Director to design and implement strategies to move donors through the investment cycle toward solicitation by identifying, qualifying, cultivating, and stewarding them. Leads the university’s legacy gifts program including oversight of the Abraham Lincoln Society which works on estate gifts, bequests, insurance beneficiaries, charitable annuities and more; and serves as the department subject matter expert on Planned Giving. Assumes the responsibility for the stewardship of the executive team and other leadership members on campus. Acts as the department’s lead campus visit officer; and works to ensure a positive and high-quality campus visit experience for visiting constituents. Works as the division liaison with the finance office and other vendors to execute paperwork and compliance of planned gifts. Writes proposals for solicitation as well as reports and other stewardship activities. Identifies and qualifies new major donors and plans giving prospects and keeps prospects in the pipeline. Acts as liaison to the campus community to ensure that relationships with major donors are being stewarded appropriately. Forecasts and sets annual department goals for securing major gifts to the University. Devises appropriate engagement strategies to strengthen relationships with potential and current major donors to the University and advises others in the office on strategic engagement as needed. Provides the leadership in organizing special engagement events specific to major donors and prospects as needed. Makes presentations to major gift constituencies about Gallaudet, its needs for private support, and related topics. Manages some sponsorship opportunities for the University with guidance from the Executive Director. Conducts personal visits and substantive actions to move prospects towards solicitation as determined in cooperation with the Executive Director on an annual basis and based on national averages and expectations. Prepares timely acknowledgement letters; and prepares acknowledgement letters as needed for the Executive Director and the President. Prepares briefing materials for donor visits; and prepares briefing materials for the Executive Director and President as necessary. Establishes and maintains a positive and supportive working relationship with co-workers and supervisors. Demonstrates a genuine commitment to belonging in the workplace and participates in activities and workshops to foster continuous learning. Serves as a mandatory reporter under Title IX; and commits to completing Title IX training and other compliance requirements on an annual basis. Prioritizes safety in the workplace as a collective responsibility for the well-being of employees and fosters a culture of safety that is integral to our success, including strict adherence to safety procedures and protocols, as well as promptly reporting any safety hazards or incidents. Performs related and other duties as needed. Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. A minimum of four years of professional experience with fundraising, marketing and communication, or a related field.  Ability to communicate in American Sign Language. Preferred Qualifications: Experience working in a higher education setting. Knowledge, Skills, And Abilities: Strong writing and organizational skills. Strong knowledge of Gallaudet and the deaf community. Strong skills with basic data management systems and basic computer applications (e.g., Word, Excel). Strong ability to synthesize and articulate the priorities of the University and match them with the values and interests of major donors and prospects. Strong willingness to understand and promote the mission, philosophy, and vision of Gallaudet University. Demonstrated experience soliciting and acquiring planned gifts. Demonstrated experience in face-to-face solicitation of meaningful gifts and familiarity with the process of major gift cultivation.  Demonstrated experience with closing of gifts of five figures or more. Demonstrated ability to work with and across University departments, outside constituents and groups. Additional Job Details: This is a revenue-funded position.    Other Important Information: The successful candidate will undergo a background check that must be cleared prior to working at Gallaudet University.   Current Employees (Regular Status and Extended Temporary) Please use the link below or the \"Find Jobs\" report directly within Workday. https://www.myworkday.com/gallaudet/d/inst/1$9925/9925$2707.htmld   Need help finding the right job? We can recommend jobs specifically for you! Create a custom Job Alert by selecting criteria that suit your career interests.   Gallaudet University is an equal opportunity employer/educational institution and does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, national origin, religion, age, hearing status, disability, genetic information, covered veteran status, marital status, personal appearance, sexual orientation, family responsibilities, matriculation, political affiliation, source of income, place of business or residence, pregnancy, childbirth, or any other unlawful basis. This policy is in compliance with Title VII of the Civil Rights Act, the Americans with Disabilities Act, the Rehabilitation Act, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, the District of Columbia Human Rights Act, and other applicable laws and applies to all procedures affecting applicants and employees including, but not necessarily limited to: recruitment, hiring, placement, promotion, transfer, reassignment, reappointment, tenure, demotion, selection for training, layoff, furlough, and termination, compensation, and all other conditions or privileges of employment."
    },
    "https://gallaudet.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/GUCareers/job/Gallaudet-University/Director--Equal-Opportunity-Programs_JR0000001353": {
      "title": "Director, Equal Opportunity Programs",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nOVERVIEW: Gallaudet University, federally chartered in 1864, is a bilingual, diverse, and multicultural institution of higher education dedicated to the intellectual and professional advancement of Deaf and hard of hearing individuals through American Sign Language and English. Gallaudet maintains a proud tradition of research and scholarly activity and prepares graduates for career opportunities in a highly competitive, technological, and rapidly changing world. As part of the university, the Laurent Clerc National Deaf Education Center is a federally funded center that operates exemplary elementary and secondary education programs for deaf and hard of hearing students as well as national outreach programs which develop and disseminate innovative curricula, instructional techniques, and products nationwide, and provide information, training, and technical assistance to parents and professionals serving children from birth through age 21.   Job Title: Director, Equal Opportunity Programs Worker Type: Regular   Pay Type: Salary   Exempt Status: Yes   Compensation Grade (Track/Level): M2   Hiring Range (Commensurate with experience and qualifications): $122,388.00-$163,184.00   Salary Ranges: All Compensation Grades   Supervisory Organization (Department): Office of Belonging and Engagement   Summary: Reporting to the Executive Director of Belonging and Engagement, the Director of Equal Opportunity Programs assumes the responsibilities for the administration, coordination, and day-to-day implementation of the University’s equal opportunity, Title IX, and disability access programs.   Job Description: Serves as the University’s Equal Opportunity Programs (EOP) Officer, Section 504 /ADA Coordinator, and Title IX Coordinator, and supports the University’s compliance with applicable federal, state, and local anti-discrimination, equal opportunity, disability, and gender-based misconduct laws and University policies. Coordinates and administers the University’s Title IX program, including its policies and procedures, and supervises the work of the Deputy Title IX Coordinator(s) and Title IX Investigator(s). Monitors Title IX compliance across University programs and activities, including admissions, recruitment, academic programs, housing, facilities, course offerings, extracurricular activities, financial assistance, student employment, and athletics, and identifies areas requiring corrective or preventive action and works with relevant stakeholders to address them. Oversees the Disability Service Center and the University’s processes for providing reasonable accommodations and access for students and campus visitors, and provides guidance and support regarding institutional compliance with applicable disability and nondiscrimination requirements. Administers the University’s processes for the prompt and appropriate resolution of discrimination, harassment, sexual misconduct, disability discrimination, and other equal opportunity complaints involving employees, students, applicants, and members of the public. Maintains case management systems, records, data, and reporting for Equal Opportunity Programs and Title IX matters, including incident reports, complaints, investigations, and other required compliance documentation. Develops and reviews investigation plans, ensuring timely completion and the inclusion of appropriate supporting documentation. Conducts and oversees investigations of alleged violations of University anti-discrimination policies and applicable laws, including Title IX sexual misconduct, sexual harassment, disability discrimination, and other discrimination claims; prepares and reviews investigative findings and reports, and recommends appropriate corrective or remedial action based on the findings, as appropriate. Makes determinations regarding allegations of disability discrimination and non-compliance under the ADA, Section 504, and other applicable federal, state, and local laws, consistent with applicable University policies and procedures and in consultation with the Office of the General Counsel, as appropriate. Develops, reviews, maintains, and updates University policies on nondiscrimination and equal opportunity, disability access and reasonable accommodations, and Title IX to reflect changes in applicable law and regulatory requirements,, and advises University units regarding related compliance requirements and concerns. Develops and coordinates training and educational resources related to nondiscrimination and Title IX, as appropriate. Provides guidance to University leadership and units regarding nondiscrimination, Title IX, disability access, and related compliance requirements and provides information and guidance to faculty, staff, students, and applicants regarding available complaint and resolution processes. Collaborates with Human Resources to identify and address nondiscrimination and equal opportunity concerns related to faculty and staff recruitment, selection, promotion, and other employment practices.. Identifies and evaluates systemic trends and concerns related to nondiscrimination and Title IX, and recommends appropriate policy, educational, or institutional responses. Participates in institutional planning and University committees, as appropriate, to address nondiscrimination, Title IX, disability access, and equal opportunity considerations. Manages the budgets for the Office of Equal Opportunity Programs, including Title IX and Disability Services Center, and ensures appropriate fiscal management and compliance with University procedures. Establishes and maintains a positive and supportive working relationship with co-workers and supervisors. Demonstrates a genuine commitment to belonging in the workplace and participates in activities and workshops to foster continuous learning. Prioritizes safety in the workplace as a collective responsibility for the well-being of employees and fosters a culture of safety that is integral to our success, including strict adherence to safety procedures and protocols, as well as promptly reporting any safety hazards or incidents. Performs related and other duties as needed. Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. A minimum of five years of professional experience with labor relations, equal opportunity compliance, human resources, or a related field. Ability to communicate in American Sign Language.  Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree. Supervisory experience. Experience working in a higher education setting. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Strong ability to gather, analyze, and evaluate facts to develop complex EEO data analyses, draw fair and reasonable conclusions, and make sound decisions. Strong knowledge of EDIB practices and federal and District of Columbia employment and employee relations laws and regulations, including EEO, ADA, Rehabilitation Act, FLSA, FMLA, Title VI, Title VII, Title IX, FERPA, and the Clery Act. Strong organizational, planning, analytical, problem-solving, reporting, record-keeping, and project management skills. Demonstrated skills with developing and delivering training to diverse audiences as well as Microsoft Office and related reporting tools. Demonstrated ability to work with and across University departments, outside constituents and groups.   Other Important Information: The successful candidate will undergo a background check that must be cleared prior to working at Gallaudet University.   Current Employees (Regular Status and Extended Temporary) Please use the link below or the \"Find Jobs\" report directly within Workday. https://www.myworkday.com/gallaudet/d/inst/1$9925/9925$2699.htmld   Need help finding the right job? We can recommend jobs specifically for you! Create a custom Job Alert by selecting criteria that suit your career interests.   Gallaudet University is an equal opportunity employer/educational institution and does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, national origin, religion, age, hearing status, disability, genetic information, covered veteran status, marital status, personal appearance, sexual orientation, family responsibilities, matriculation, political affiliation, source of income, place of business or residence, pregnancy, childbirth, or any other unlawful basis. This policy is in compliance with Title VII of the Civil Rights Act, the Americans with Disabilities Act, the Rehabilitation Act, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, the District of Columbia Human Rights Act, and other applicable laws and applies to all procedures affecting applicants and employees including, but not necessarily limited to: recruitment, hiring, placement, promotion, transfer, reassignment, reappointment, tenure, demotion, selection for training, layoff, furlough, and termination, compensation, and all other conditions or privileges of employment."
    },
    "https://gallaudet.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/GUCareers/job/Gallaudet-University/Financial-Services-Coordinator_JR0000001352": {
      "title": "Financial Services Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nOVERVIEW: Gallaudet University, federally chartered in 1864, is a bilingual, diverse, and multicultural institution of higher education dedicated to the intellectual and professional advancement of Deaf and hard of hearing individuals through American Sign Language and English. Gallaudet maintains a proud tradition of research and scholarly activity and prepares graduates for career opportunities in a highly competitive, technological, and rapidly changing world. As part of the university, the Laurent Clerc National Deaf Education Center is a federally funded center that operates exemplary elementary and secondary education programs for deaf and hard of hearing students as well as national outreach programs which develop and disseminate innovative curricula, instructional techniques, and products nationwide, and provide information, training, and technical assistance to parents and professionals serving children from birth through age 21.   Job Title: Financial Services Coordinator Worker Type: Regular   Pay Type: Salary   Exempt Status: Yes   Compensation Grade (Track/Level): P2   Hiring Range (Commensurate with experience and qualifications): $70,878.00-$94,469.00   Salary Ranges: All Compensation Grades   Supervisory Organization (Department): Student Financial Services   Summary: Reporting to the Manager of Student Financial Services (SFS), the Financial Services Coordinator coordinates student account activities, focusing on Vocational Rehabilitation (VR), third-party sponsorships, billing, receivables, account reconciliation, and discrepancy resolution, while collaborating with University departments to ensure accurate financial transactions, maintain compliance, provide quality service, and improve operational processes.   Job Description: Vocational Rehabilitation and Third-Party Billing Serves as the primary Student Financial Services (SFS) point of contact for Vocational Rehabilitation (VR) agencies and other third-party sponsors, maintaining effective working relationships and coordinating billing and payment activities. Reviews authorizations and sponsor requirements to verify eligibility, covered charges, funding limitations, and billing conditions; and prepares invoices and monitors outstanding receivables. Processes and reconciles third-party payments, credits, refunds, and adjustments; and researches and resolves discrepancies involving authorizations, invoices, charges, and balances. Maintains accurate supporting documentation and recommends improvements to billing and collection processes. Student Account Coordination and Collaboration Reviews, analyzes, and reconciles student accounts to resolve discrepancies involving charges, payments, financial aid, sponsorships, waivers, credits, refunds, and adjustments. Collaborates with the University departments to resolve complex account issues and coordinate the accurate application of financial aid and third-party funding. Provides guidance on student account procedures, billing, sponsorships, payment processes, and financial clearance requirements. Reporting, Compliance, and Process Improvement Develops and maintains reports on third-party billing, authorizations, invoices, payments, receivables, and student account activity; and analyzes data to identify discrepancies, trends, and opportunities for improvement. Maintains records and supporting documentation for audits and ensures compliance with University policies, applicable regulations, contractual requirements, and SFS procedures. Supports process documentation, workflow improvements, and testing of Workday Student, Transact, and other student financial services systems. Student and Sponsor Services Provides timely, professional assistance to students, families, VR counselors, sponsors, and University employees regarding student accounts, billing, funding, and financial responsibilities through email, phone, video, in-person service, and ServiceNow. Explains account information and financial requirements clearly, helps resolve funding and payment issues, and ensures consistent application of University policies and financial controls. Additional Responsibilities Provides backup support for SFS operations and participates in projects, committees, training, orientations, and outreach activities as assigned. Establishes and maintains a positive and supportive working relationship with co-workers and supervisors. Demonstrates a genuine commitment to belonging in the workplace and participates in activities and workshops to foster continuous learning. Serves as a mandatory reporter under Title IX; and commits to completing Title IX training and other compliance requirements on an annual basis. Prioritizes safety in the workplace as a collective responsibility for the well-being of employees and fosters a culture of safety that is integral to our success, including strict adherence to safety procedures and protocols, as well as promptly reporting any safety hazards or incidents.   Performs related and other duties as needed. Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. A minimum of three years of professional experience with student services, financial analysis, or a related field. Ability to communicate in American Sign Language. Preferred Qualifications: Experience working with Vocational Rehabilitation (VR) agencies. Experience working in a higher education setting. Knowledge, Skills, And Abilities: Strong ability to analyze and reconcile financial transactions, resolve discrepancies, maintain accuracy, and manage multiple priorities and deadlines. Strong ability to communicate effectively and provide excellent customer service to students, families, external agencies, and University departments. Strong ability to collaborate effectively with students, families, external agencies, and University departments in support of timely and accurate financial services. Demonstrated experience with Vocational Rehabilitation (VR) billing, third-party sponsorships, student accounts, billing, payments, refunds, accounts receivable, or higher education financial operations. Demonstrated experience using Workday Student, Transact, PeopleSoft, or another higher education ERP, as well as Microsoft Excel and Microsoft Office. Demonstrated ability to work with and across university departments, outside constituents and groups.   Other Important Information: The successful candidate will undergo a background check that must be cleared prior to working at Gallaudet University.   Current Employees (Regular Status and Extended Temporary) Please use the link below or the \"Find Jobs\" report directly within Workday. https://www.myworkday.com/gallaudet/d/inst/1$9925/9925$2697.htmld   Need help finding the right job? We can recommend jobs specifically for you! Create a custom Job Alert by selecting criteria that suit your career interests.   Gallaudet University is an equal opportunity employer/educational institution and does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, national origin, religion, age, hearing status, disability, genetic information, covered veteran status, marital status, personal appearance, sexual orientation, family responsibilities, matriculation, political affiliation, source of income, place of business or residence, pregnancy, childbirth, or any other unlawful basis. This policy is in compliance with Title VII of the Civil Rights Act, the Americans with Disabilities Act, the Rehabilitation Act, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act, the District of Columbia Human Rights Act, and other applicable laws and applies to all procedures affecting applicants and employees including, but not necessarily limited to: recruitment, hiring, placement, promotion, transfer, reassignment, reappointment, tenure, demotion, selection for training, layoff, furlough, and termination, compensation, and all other conditions or privileges of employment."
    }
